title: v6.6.1 follow-up — Arky, Topo, Atlas collision resolved + roster advertised in README
summary: |-
  Three small but real cleanups on top of v6.6.0. Architect got a proper nickname (Arky — previously the placeholder string "architect"). Cartographer was added to the bundle as Topo, fixing the 68th-archetype gap (it was tier-1 in AGENT_TIERS but had no .agent file). The long-standing Atlas-name collision was resolved in cartographer's favor of giving up the name — Atlas now belongs solely to devops; cartographer is Topo. The 68-agent roster is now advertised in the top-level README, grouped by model tier with the sync-global CTA.
symbols_touched:
  - '#agents'
  - '#agent-identity'
  - '#orchestration'
  - '#docs'
learnings:
  - Naming collisions can persist invisibly across surfaces — orchestration.ts referred to cartographer as ATLAS while devops.agent owned the Atlas nickname in the bundle. Same name, different definitions, no compile-time check caught it. Worth a follow-up audit pass (nickname uniqueness validator at load time) to make collisions hard errors.
  - Authoring a new bundled agent inline (without spawning Loid) was tractable here because the orchestration ATLAS_PROMPT already specified the cartographer's role and behaviors — the .agent file just needed to mirror that as a rich description. Loid-pass would still be the right move for a from-scratch persona.
  - The README is high-leverage real estate for showcasing what ships. Going from "core 8 + 3 specialists" prose to a 68-agent tier-grouped roster materially changes how new users perceive the framework's depth, with near-zero maintenance cost.
